  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCold front clears the coast late this afternoon with cooler and\ndrier air filtering in on gusty NW winds.\n\nQuiet weather will dominate the pattern Tuesday, Wednesday, and\nmuch of Thanksgiving Day. It will be cooler, with highs mainly \nin the lower 30s to lower 40s during the afternoons. We could \nsee widespread lows in the teens Wednesday and Thursday \nmornings which would be the coldest of the season thus far.\n\nThe next coastal storm then approaches late in the day on\nThursday from the southwest. There is good agreement that as the\nprimary low moves northward into the eastern Great Lakes, a\nsecondary low will develop along a warm front near Long Island\nNY Thursday night, then move northeastward into the Gulf of ME\nor along the immediate ME coast Friday. This will leave the bulk\nof the forecast area in the cool sector of the system as we'll\nbe jammed in with CAD owing to the high pressure system to our\nnorth. This will mean a cold rain for most folks Thu night into\nFri. However, the bulk of the heaviest precipitation looks to\nfall mainly in a 6 hour period at any given location Thu night\nand Fri morning. Lighter precipitation will hang around through\nthe day on Friday and move out either Friday night or Saturday \nmorning. A brief wintry mix with sleet and some freezing rain \nis also not out of the question for some areas in and around the\nfoothills and mountains.\n\nWinds on land should behave themselves with the low level CAD in\nplace, i.e., strong low level inversion. However, if the low\npressure system were to move northward slightly inland from the\ncoast, then the warm sector could move onto the coastal plain\nand allow strong easterly winds to mix down late Thu night or\nFri morning.\n\nGiven the trends for strong easterly flow on the coastal waters,\ncoastal flooding is a concern, mainly for the Friday midday high\ntide. It looks like winds and seas won't ramp up fast enough to\ncause widespread coastal flood problems Thanksgiving Day.\nHowever with very high astro tides, pockets of minor flooding\nwill be possible.\n\nNon-impactful weather looks to be the norm Sunday and Monday.",
